---
// The engine: provider calls that scale out (the fan-out figure), the rest of the engine with
// Restate named in Restate's own words (D18, revised 2026-10-18), the console as a preview, and
// state encryption as HCL. Sources: /compatibility/'s strengths table, /engine/'s engine list, the
// encrypted fixture in doof's v1 test suite.
import FanOutFigure from './figures/FanOutFigure.astro';
import Still from './Still.astro';
---

<section class="band alt">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="band-head">
      <h2>Built to run the whole stack.</h2>
      <p>One engine for every stack on this page, on a laptop or as a service in the cluster.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="feature center fig-lg">
      <div>
        <FanOutFigure />
        <p class="figcap"><b>Shipped:</b> every refresh, plan and apply call fans out from one engine, with a
          provider instance per region, account or alias. <span class="pill soon">coming soon</span> more
          engine nodes and per-provider flow control, to reach each API’s quota without tripping it.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Every provider call scales out. The cloud API is the ceiling.</h3>
        <p>Refresh reads every managed resource on every plan, and in one process it is the classic
          ceiling on a large stack.</p>
        <p>Turf fans refresh, plan and apply out per resource across the engine, with durable
          per-resource state instead of one statefile under one lock. <b>Thousands of resources per
          stack</b>; the cloud API, not the tool, sets the pace. Per-provider flow control
          <span class="pill soon">coming soon</span> paces each provider to its own quota.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="feature flip">
      <div>
        <h3>Durable, observable, yours to run.</h3>
        <ul class="engine-list one">
          <li><b>Durable runs.</b> Every step is recorded as it happens; an interrupted run picks up where
            it stopped, a plan waiting for approval holds no process, and a rollout outlives the
            conversation that started it.</li>
          <li><b>Laptop or Kubernetes®.</b> Runs on a workstation, or as a service in the cluster.</li>
          <li><b>OpenTelemetry built in.</b> Every run traced as it happens, exported over OTLP; metrics
            in Prometheus format.</li>
          <li><b>Encrypted at rest.</b> State encryption with a passphrase or a key.</li>
          <li><b>Operator console.</b> Every stack, run and approval in one place.
            <span class="pill soon">coming soon</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p>The engine runs on <a href="https://restate.dev">Restate</a>, in Restate’s words “a lightweight
          runtime to turn AI agents, workflows, and backend services into durable processes.”
          <a href="/engine/#engine">How the engine is built →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <Still id="console" src="/console/aicr-converged.png" soon
          alt="A preview of the Turf console: a deployment run in two rounds, converged, with a lane for every resource of the stack."
          caption="A preview of the Turf console: every stack, run and approval in one place. Click to enlarge." />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="feature center">
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>versions.tf</span><span class="cb-legend">state encrypted at rest</span></div>
<pre><span class="kw">terraform</span> &#123;
  <span class="kw">encryption</span> &#123;
    <span class="kw">key_provider</span> <span class="str">"pbkdf2"</span> <span class="str">"k"</span> &#123;
      passphrase = var.passphrase
    &#125;
    <span class="kw">method</span> <span class="str">"aes_gcm"</span> <span class="str">"m"</span> &#123;
      keys = key_provider.pbkdf2.k
    &#125;
    <span class="kw">state</span> &#123;
      method   = method.aes_gcm.m
      enforced = true
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</pre>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Encrypted at rest, enforced.</h3>
        <p>An <code>encryption</code> block protects the statefile with a passphrase or a key provider;
          migrating an existing state is a no-op run, and <code>enforced</code> refuses plaintext. A
          secret a resource needs goes through a write-only argument and never reaches state at all.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
